  "summary": "Sequence foundation models are moving from generic pretraining toward specialized inputs, diagnostic evaluation, and enhancer design.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is the adaptation of DNA sequence foundation models to narrower biological inputs and tasks. An apicomplexan-specific model pretrained on 455 assemblies improves regulatory signal prediction in data-sparse small genomes of Plasmodium falciparum, while ModBERT incorporates population variants and long-read CpG methylation to predict chromatin features and variant effects. Other preprints test what these models actually learn: LAMP examines why fine-tuning on personal genomes does not improve gene expression prediction, and fine-tuned Borzoi and AlphaGenome perform comparably to variant-based methods for whole-blood expression in admixed Latino individuals. On the design side, GENECraft couples a genomic language model with a functional oracle without training, and REGAL uses reinforcement learning over regulatory grammar to generate tissue-specific enhancers validated across species. What is new is the shift from generic pretraining toward specialized inputs, diagnostic evaluation, and constrained regulatory design.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-21"
}
